Don't take this as an official statement, but I understand that
"uname" will change to return "UNIX" (I'm not sure about all-upper-case).
I assume that there will always be software that cares if it is running
on "The UNIX System Formerly Known as DEC OSF/1"; I've asked the product
people whether there will be any way to use uname to distinguish Digital
UNIX from other UNIX systems.

Of course, when "uname -sm" returns "UNIX alpha", then presumably
it is running on "The UNIX System Formerly Known as DEC OSF/1".  I suppose
it might be Linux/Alpha or NetBSD/Alpha, but I doubt that Linux or NetBSD
will be interested in becoming licensed to call themselves UNIX.

Note that the C preprocessor will presumably continue to define
__osf__, so this program
	main() {
	#if	defined(__osf__) && defined(__alpha)
		printf("The UNIX System Formerly Known as DEC OSF/1\n");
	#else
		printf("Some other UNIX System\n");
	#endif
	}
should work.

