Organization: Computer Science Department, Southwest Texas State University
Lines: 36
Message-ID: <3l0db8$5b0@pirates.cs.swt.edu>
NNTP-Posting-Host: pirates.cs.swt.edu
X-Newsreader: TIN [version 1.2 PL2]

A while back, I posted requests about information how to set
SLIP/PPP connection from a Mac PPC to a DEC Alpha AXP box.

I didn't receive many replies with solutions.  Instead, I did
received a lot of inquiries asking what information I obtained.
Here is a summary of what I have done.

1. SLIP.  I slightly hacked the "sliplogin" subset in cslip-2.7
   and made it working under OSF3.0/3.2.  Proxyarp does not work
   (this is part of the well-known "chroot" problem under DEC OSF),
   but a hand entered "arp" entry for my Mac on the AXP works fine.
   Compression does not work, but my modems do compressions so it
   is hardly a problem.  I have put a package for anonymous FTP:
	ftp://pirates.cs.swt.edu/pub/networking/cslip-2.7/sliplogin.tar

2. Steve Tate from UNT hacked ppp-2.1.2.  I tested it and it's
   working great under both OSF3.0 and 3.2.  Unlike SLIP, which is
   built into the kernel in OSF3.0 and above,  one has to rebuild
   the kernel to have the PPP support.  Steve may be still
   not ready yet to make it available to the public.  But it should
   be very soon.

Compared the two,  PPP seems 10-15% faster than SLIP.  So I am using
PPP right now.

I just hope that this kind of information can be put into the FAQ.
Also hope that PPP support will become a standard part of the
OSF/DEC UNIX.  SUN has done a much better job in this regard.  I
posted an article about the proxyarp/compression/mtu problems that
I have with sliplogin before,  and nobody from DEC ever answered.
I supposed that UNIX people from DEC are reading this newsgroup.

--

--wuxu peng (peng@pirates.cs.swt.edu)



