I cannot speak to the Pinnacle drive, but if you can get it to identify
as a SCSI device type 0 (= hard disk) rather than type 7(optical), and
if you can get media with 512 byte sectors, you can almost certainly
make it work with OSF/1.  I have an HP C1716T working under OSF/1, but
I was frustrated at every turn until I got a hardware manual for it
and used the "scu" program to change a setting in the drive's
non-volatile RAM so that it would identify as a hard disk.  Once that
was done, it was just like setting up any other third party disk,
except that you have to put a disklabel on each new cartridge.


