In article <1995Mar22.142836.15049@govonca.gov.on.ca>,
Teresa Wawrzyczek <wawrzyt@govonca.gov.on.ca> wrote:
> Can anyone tell me please if it is possible to spawn OSF commands from C
> and how to do that?

Easiest:

	system(command);

    Eg: system("ls -l");

Most control:

	#define I_FAILED some failure value you want to use 1..255

	int pid;
	int status;
	void *intval;

	intval = signal(SIGINT, SIG_IGN); /* ignore ^C while child is running */
	                                  /* add other signals here */
	switch(pid = fork()) {	/* create a child process */
	  case -1: /* an error occurred, no child exists */
	    perror("fork");	/* tell the user */
			return I_FAILED; /* tell the rest of the program */
	  case 0: /* This is the side of the fork in the child */
	    signal(SIGINT, intval); /* let child handle sigs */
	    execlp("command", "command_name", "arg1", ..., (char *)NULL);
	    perror("command"); /* If you got here, something's wrong */
	    exit(I_FAILED); /* Tell the parent */
	  default: /* This is the side of the fork in the parent */
	    waitpid(pid, &status, 0); /* wait for the child to die */
	    signal(SIGINT, intval); /* listen to our own signals */
	    return status;
	}

Read the man pages for the various routines until you understand what this
code does. It's really quite a clever mechanism.
