No, but it can't be too hard to find.  The system does not start
swapping for no reason at all.  It swaps in response to a demand for
more memory.  Do you have a copy of the public domain 'top' program?
One site to try is ftp://eecs.nwu.edu/pub/top/.  This should show
you what is running, and how big it is.  

I use a 3.2 system that is very overcommitted (lots more virtual
address space in use than physical memory).   When it starts to
swap it is quite aggressive about it, but I've never seen it
start to swap without me running some big program that forces
other things out of memory.

