grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu (Dirk Grunwald) wrote:
>
> ST> Well, we have an RS/6000-590, and a collection of DEC 3000/600's. We
> --
>
> But then again, a 3000/600 is a fairly old & slow machine, by todays
> standards, and a fair-sight cheaper than the RS6000/590. We had some
> people comparing a 2100 with 275Mhz cpu's and found them to be about
> the same as a 590, per cpu
>
> But, they could get 2 CPU's for the same money.

Hi,

If this is an application server or similar I'd go for the
multiple CPUs.  If we're looking at a number-crunching machine
with unthreaded code then one could also argue that multiple
CPUs can support twice the load before degrading to the
same extent as a single CPU machine (ignoring i/o etc).

Frankly the SPARC 20/61(2) or SPARC 20/71(2) does not really
hold out against the bigger DEC, HP, and IBM machines as
a really-high-end desktop or compile server for a limited number of users.
But they do perform well for not-too-CPU-intensive tasks
that simply require high throughput through CPU availability.
We recently installed SPARCserver 612's with 128Mb running
a library application for a bunch of online users.  The
application vendor is of the opinion that these machines
had a much higher throughput than the IBM 590 when it came
to supporting users and at the same time re-indexing the
entire database that had just been moved from the old
platform.  Similarly, a SPARCserver 20/712 can achive over
1000 TPM-C---much more than many far more expensive machines
that are better off for a small number of high-power users.
Hopefully UltraSPARC should put Sun back in the top-flight
for desktops so that they don't just win on price/performance
ratio.

Just my 1/2-cent

Gavin



