Alan Simon (simon@shrsys.hslc.org) wrote:
: I know that the export command passes variables from the shell down to
: subshells.  How do I pass a variable from a subshell back up to the original
: shell?

Strictly speaking, you can't do that in UNIX.  However, you can have the
subshell echo a value to standard output, and store the output in a
variable in the parent shell using back-quotes.  For example,
in the parent shell:

	...
	VALUE=`myscript arg1 arg2 ...`
	echo VALUE is $VALUE
	...

and in 'myscript':
	...
	echo "Return value"
	exit 0

Or, just send different exit codes back to the parent, and test
the $status variable (cshell) or $? variable (bourne shell).
Hope this helps.
