In article <D5rEKB.969@news.cern.ch>, solovian@axcrna.cern.ch (Oleg Solovianov) writes:
- What *is* obvious also, that most of the leading computer
- companies like Digital,HP,IBM,SGI are very close
- to each other in terms of a raw power. That's a good thing
- to my mind. So if someone have any addictions to any brand
- name, he could find out a computer with he/she likes,
- without worry that it will be 2-3 times slow that the
- ones from competing companies. But still, lots of people
- still playing silly games like perf/buck, perf/MHz, etc, etc...

Well, nice to see a thread still going after coming back from a holiday...
And then with a contribution that actually makes sense:

   Buy the machine where you like the color of the casing :-)

In fact, SPEC marks have done their thing, in roughly equalizing 
(SPEC)-performance at a given price. As such they are quite reliable.

In real life workstations run one (or several) background processes and
an X-Window front process. And the best and most reliable (and viciously
discriminating) benchmark is to find out at what level and size of 
background jobs the X-console user walks away in disgust. 

Thus, having the cpu running 100% on a tiny program that fits easily
in the processor cache (500 bytes, say), and is running at slightly 
lower priority (nice 4, say, as some shells do automatically when 
spawning background jobs) will not impact the console user. But what 
happens when a background program does moderate programmed I/O (i.e. 
(f)read/(f)write), say 50 kbyte/second, or page I/O (i.e. virtual 
memory access), say 20 Pages/second ?

On some machines you just don't notice any degradation, others become 
unuseable for editing or reading mail. 

Then there is the size of compiled programs, which has to be considered
not only for disk space usage, but also for response time purposes.
Subjectively this can be seen by walking up to a machine and typing
"date" or some such trivial command, but it really hits you when you
are in a class with 20 workstations or so hanging off a file server 
and you ask people to compile a simple program or start an appliction.

To take the compiled binary for mathematica (.../Bin/mathexe, stripped
in all cases) as an example:

    Sunos 4.1.3            6440656 Jul 11  1994 mathexe
    OSF1 V2.0             25026560 Jun  3  1994 mathexe
    AIX 3.2                6583062 Jun  1  1994 mathexe
    IRIX 5.3               7249984 Nov 15 11:09 mathexe

This kind of code has to cross the network as well as the cache, and with
a 1.2 Mbyte/second Ethernet and 20 machines requesting the same code,
you can work out the startup times ...
 
Unfortunately the "get everything into memory and it will run like a bat
out of hell" approach is not good enough for real-life budgets.

