I don't know the characteristic of your application, but we realized
that especially the R8000 depends extremely on the code you feed it
with (comparable to a vector computer). For a finite element code on an
unstructured grid I could not get a speedup of more than a factor of
2.5 (without changing the code! this should be better if we had the
time to tune the code for the R8000) compared to an R4000 100 MHz
Indigo! But the Cray C90 showed no speedup at all!!! The execution time
was nearly identical to those on 100 MHz R4000s.

For a finite difference code, optimized for running on a Cray
vectorizing computer the speedup is much better than that. For smaller
grid sizes (which could be influenced by the presence of caches) it lies
at about 6 for the R8000 and 17 for the Cray:

R4000:     100 timesteps, 21*21*49:       1:45   -O3 -sopt -mips2
R8000:     100 timesteps, 21*21*49:       0:17   -O3 -sopt (64 bit default)
Cray C90:  100 timesteps, 21*21*49:       0:06   -O vector2 -O scalar2

For a larger grid, we get the following numbers:

R4000:	     1 timestep, 55*65*75:   cancelled after 40 minutes!!
R8000:       1 timestep, 55*65*75:   0:29
Cray C90:    1 timestep, 55*65*75:   0:05

The improved ratio between Cray and R8000 might be related to the better
utilization of the vectorregisters due to the longer array lengths. Or
to growing over the cache size on the PowerIndigo.

There may be a problem with the R4000 for the large grid, since the
gr_osview bar shows a significant portion of system activity (red)
which we cannot explain; this behaviour does not happen with smaller
grid sizes (e.g. 21*21*49) and was seen on an R4000 Indigo with 5.3 as
well as on an R4400 Indigo2 with 5.2; the Indigo2 showed 170 of 256 MB
free during the computation, so swapping cannot be the reason for this
behaviour. Maybe this is a completely different problem, which should
be discussed on its own (I just ran into it). But even if we loose a
factor of four due to some other problem (the CPU bar is ~25% blue),
the speedup still may be 6 or larger, comparing R4000 and R8000).

So, if you have a code with a certain structure of computations and
data organization this might run very well on one architecture, but not
so well on another. Or the other way round: a given architecture boosts
one numerical code, while it might even slow down another code. In the
finite difference scheme on the regular grid, the presence of large
arrays of data to which the same operation is applied suits very well
the vectorizing Crays and the superscalar R8000. The more or less
random data distribution of the finite element scheme on the
unstructured grid makes it nearly impossible to keep the vector unit or
superscalar pipeline busy all the time, thus reducing the performance
significantly.

Also the presence of integer operations can distort the floating point
flow to the FPU (this is not directly related to the ratio of integer
vs.  floating point operations: few integer operations at the wrong
time can distort the execution flow of the floating point operations
drastically). If you take a look into the R8000 porting guide you will
find examples, where loops are unrolled by hand, index computations
done explicitly, thus allowing the compiler to insert the addresses at
compile time in opposition to calculating them during the execution.
The precalculated addresses make it possible to feed the FPU with a
higher rate instead of having interruptions caused by the
index-calulations of the integer unit.

The field of benchmarks is so complex, that I normally use the numbers
just as a hint to what I might expect. For everyday use, the system bus
and disk-speed are very important too (and not contained in many
benchmarks) and for the special case of numerical computations the cases
stated above show clearly that you have to bring your own application to
the CPUs in question to be able to make the best choice.

There are no miracles out there :-)

Andreas

