I've been watching this problem off and on, through 3 versions of
OSF/1 on 3 different machines in different parts of the country and
it's starting to really annoy me.

Basically, on very high volume, long duration tcp links between
osf/1 machines and so far hpux, aix, and at least one sun, I and two
other people I  know, have expereienced what appears to be tcp breaking
at the kernel level or something.  Symptoms are as follows.
1. bi directional data between two identical applications on cross
  country links.  Used to be all ANS, now it's mostly sprintlink.. etc (point
  is long distance routes have no measurable impact if changed)
2. Two applications send upwards of 2Kbytes/sec continuously with
  much higher peaks.
3. Non-OSF/1 side stops talking to OSF/1 side..
   - OSF/1 side continues to write, non-osf/1 side continues to read
     but not the other way around
   - netstat on alpha shows connection status ESTABLISHED
   - netstat on hpux/aix/sunos side shows ESTABLISHED
   - no FIN_WAIT or FIN_anything for that matter.
   - A select() call on the specific fd times out.. thinks nothing to read
4.  A few minutes later application level on alpha notices it's no
   receiving anything so it closes the connection, it thinks, normally.
   - netstat on alpha is clean.  No FIN_* or anything like it.
5. hpukes/aix/sunos side does not close connection.  It doesn't even
   notice that anything has happened.
   - netstat there shows that it still thinks the connection is established
6. some period of time after alpha has closed connection other end
   appplication level notices no activity and closes the connection at
   its end.

They reconnect.. and process repeats itself.  Sometimes as much as twice an
hours.. sometimes only twice a day.  The order is always the same.. the link
always fails in only one direction.. Local and remote routers and networks
have changed over the course of the 2 years I've noticed the problem and
the symptoms and timing are consistently uneffected.

Notable facts:
- It does not happen ever when alpha talks to same application on
  another alpha or dec ultrix box.
- It does not appear to happen if alpha is doing all of the sending and
  other end is only listening.
- Related to the last, the volume of data appears to be somehow relevant.
  Same application, same type of hosts, but less traffic (1/10th)  and it
  doesn't happen as much.  And it doesn't appear to happen with stuff like
  telnet ever.
- It does not happen between the hpux/sunos/aix and other
  hpux/sunos/aix/ultrix/dgux/solaris machines that run the same application.
- I have, in the past, run continuous pings between the alpha and the remote
  machine through the entire period from before it breaks to after, and
  shown 0 packet loss.  That's not always the case, but I generally see no
  increase in packet loss around the period of the problem.

The only common factor appears to be the alpha, but that does not explain
why the OTHER end breaks at the network level, after the alpha has closed
the connection.  Also, as mentioned before, mine is not the only OSF/1 box
that has seen this problem.  I've watched it through V1.3 1.3X 2.0 and 3.0
and it remains a constant irritation.

I've asked a lot of people about it and received a lot of answers which
always point the blame away from whichever part of the system they think
they understand the most.  At this point, most people would have me
believe, that it's not happening 10-20 times a day.

If anyone has any realistic ideas as to what might cause the above problem
and how to fix or avoid it, I'd love to know.  If anyone else has SEEN
the problem I'd like to know as well.

	ian

