MacLure wrote in article <3k9n46$j5i@onramp.arc.nasa.gov> :
>We have an ALPHA sitting in our office with something like 300Meg RAM.
>The amount of paging that goes on is more a function of the speed of the
>chip and cache size than memory. 

I think not.  Paging is primarily a function of how much memory your
applications demand (the working set size) versus how much memory your
system has.  The only relationship I can think of between CPU speed and
paging is that a faster CPU can issue more references to memory per
unit time, and these references may or may not be satisfied in the
cache or main memory.  But this is why you buy a fast computer:
you want more work done (or at least attempted) per unit time.

>                                 There is a drawback to the screamingly
>high clock rates that some companies use in that if you "blow" cache your 
>system performance takes an enormous hit. 

The other way to state this is that while the workload stays in the
cache you get amazing performance, and once it falls out of the cache
you get the same performance that almost everyone else does, because
almost everyone uses similar main memory and IO designs these days.

>IBM, for instance has chosen to get their performance increments by
>reworking the S/W and running the chips at quite modest speeds ( at
>least for the RS6000 ). If and when they choose to start boosting the
>clock rates, performance will increase dramatically.

IBM's clock rate has stayed modest because they implement their POWER
CPUs with multiple (between 6 and 9) chips.  The clock rate has stayed
modest because it takes longer for signals to travel between the
multiple chips.  With the PowerPC single-chip CPUs IBM will be
be increasing CPU speeds.

