fredc@ncms.org (Fred Champlain) writes:

>We are currently running our OSF 3.0 systems using ufs.  We are considering 
>changing them over to the POLYCENTER advfs.   Are there any caveats to look 
>out for in doing this?   What kind of performance increase does advfs provide?

When you move to advfs you have to use vdump and vrestore rather than
dump and restore.  Be aware that vdump and vrestore have a couple of
problems.  They don't understand user@host: when working to a remote
system, and restore gets its interactive input from primary input.
This makes it impossible to pipe the data stream to a vrestore -i,
since it would try to read the interactive commands from the pipe.

These aren't necessarily serious problems.  But if you expect to be
able to use existing dump and restore scripts just be replacing
dump/restore with vdump/vrestore, they could come as a shock.



