Dave Cherkus (dc>) and Dan Pop (dp>) have written:

dc> The other way to state this is that while the workload stays in the
dc> cache you get amazing performance, and once it falls out of the cache
dc> you get the same performance that almost everyone else does, because
dc> almost everyone uses similar main memory and IO designs these days.

dp> Not quite.  If you have a 150 MHz box and 60 MHz one, both delivering
dp> the same performace when running inside the cache, the 150 MHz will be
dp> considerably slower than the 60 MHz one when they fall out of the cache.

Dan, could you help me understand what you're saying here?  I tried to
construct an example, but couldn't make it work the way you describe.

Suppose a program consists of two sections.  Call the first section "A";
for concreteness, assume it has 300M simple operations and works
entirely out of the cache.  Since you have said that both machines
deliver the same performance, let's assume that the 150Mhz system can do
dual-issue and the 60Mhz system can do 5-way issue.  For simplicity, we
further assume that the instruction mix is perfectly amenable to either
dual-issue or quin-issue.  So, both systems take 1 second to execute
section A of the program.

The other section, call it "B", is memory-bound.  For concreteness,
let's  assume that B has load instructions for 12.5M contiguous 8-byte
items that are brought in from memory, and for simplicity let's assume B
does nothing else other than load these items.  Both machines are using
similar memory technology (ex Cherkus); assume that's 100 MB/sec. 
Again, both take 1 second for this section of the program.

Now, perhaps you meant that the RELATIVE falloff is much worse for the
150Mhz system when it enters into section B: it issues only 12.5M load
instructions for 150Mhz clock ticks, whereas the 60Mhz system issues
12.5M load instructions in 60M clock ticks.  That's true.  But the
number of "issue slots" is the same: the 150Mhz dual-issue system has
300M opportunities to issue instructions per second, and the 60Mhz
quin-issue system also has 300M opportunities.

I am not necessarily saying you are wrong.  But with the simple examples
that come to mind off-hand, I don't see what you mean.

