Well, I guess I see that you are saying that running at memory speed is
a *relatively* larger loss for a fast machine than for a slower machine.
One could say that a faster machine benefits MORE from a nice, fast, big
cache than a slower machine; and so if you have a cache which is too
small then sure the fast machine has "lost" more than the slow one. 

But this is a relative loss, not a loss in absolute terms.  To use an
analogy: just because both a sportscar and a mini-van are stuck in the
same mid-town traffic, and both have the same slow performance, that
does not mean that the sportscar takes longer to cross town than the
mini-van.  They take the same amount of time.   (I will admit that it's
probably more frustrating to sit in city traffic in a sportscar.)

One last item just to be clear, the concrete example you picked is
probably NOT an example that runs in the cache.  The 3300LX has a 256Kb
board-level-cache; the 712/60 has (I think) 128KB board-level-cache
(64+64); SPEC92 cache hit rates are not so high with 256KB or 128KB. 
You are probably seeing a lot of memory effect in the example you
picked.

Hope this helps

