In article <3kcdo9$f65@news.csie.nctu.edu.tw>, cuma@csie.ncu.edu.tw (Yi-Kai Tsai) writes:
|>J-L Risler (risler@cgmvax.cgm.cnrs-gif.fr) wrote:
|>: In article <3k74fs$3ig@taco.cc.ncsu.edu>, eric_morgan@ncsu.edu says...
|>: >Do I need to install something special in the OS in order to 
|>: >mount CDROMs?
|>: -- 
|>: I had the very same problem after upgrading from v2.0 to v3.0: in the 
|>: file /sys/conf/MYMACHINE, the "option CDFS" had disappeared ... I had to 
|>: edit the file, add the option and rebuild the kernel.
|>: It is somewhat surprising that the option CDFS is not a default since the 
|>: OS is delivered on CD-ROMs......

While the operating system is delivered on CD-ROM, it does not use the ISO 9660
compact disk file system.  It uses the UNIX file system (ufs) instead.

|>
|>  if you use "upgrade" , CDFS will not be exist in kernel config file ,
|>  
|>  if you directly "install" , CDFS will be eixst in it ..
|>

If you do a full installation, you are given the option to select the CDFS
option, along with other options.  If you don't select it, you obviously don't
get it.

The situation with update installations is that it doesn't use the existing
configuration file.  There's no telling what might have been added to this file
which could cause kernel build failures during the update to the new operating
system.  For that reason, a new, clean config file is created and your old one
saved.  Also, an approach was taken to make the update process as
non-interactive as possible - that's why you don't get a chance to pick kernel
options like CDFS.

We're aware of this situation and are looking into solutions that will allow for
some level of flexibility without overly complicating the update process.

Dave


