In article <9MAR95.15052809@swdev.si.com>, tillman@swdev.si.com (Brian Tillman)
 writes:
|>Was the Fortran 77 compiler for VMS a separate product from the Fortran 66
|>compiler?  No.  Was DEC Fortran a separate product from VAX Fortran?  No.  Why
|>this change of policy?  Fortran is Fortran.  Anyone who has a valid maintenance
|>contract for Fortran should get whatever enhancements Digital makes to Fortran.
|>This business of renaming a compiler and calling it a new product has already
|>hurt Digital (recall the VAX C to DEC C fiasco).  Why doesn't Digital learn?
|>How many times does it have to shoot itself in the foot before it realizes it's
|>going to run out of toes?

If the situation was as you described it, I could certainly understand your
displeasure, but it is not.  DEC Fortran 90 is not just an enhancement and
renaming of our existing DEC Fortran (F77) compiler - it is completely new.

The difference between F90 and F77 is much larger than that between F77 and
F66.  F77's changes were largely existing practice, F90 largely consisted
of features not implemented anywhere.  At the time that VAX FORTRAN became
a full F77 implementation, it was already very close to F77.

We looked hard at the idea of taking our existing DEC Fortran compiler and
turning it into an F90 compiler, but decided that it was unworkable.  We had
acquired a F90 front end from Compass and were able to use that as a basis
for a good F90 compiler in less time than it would have taken us to rewrite
our F77 compiler, which has pieces that date back to the mid-1970s. 

We really would like to have DEC Fortran 90 be a replacement for DEC Fortran
(F77).  We'd need to fill in some missing features before we could do that
without complaints from our customers.  This is a goal we are working towards.
