In article <3jq47h$1n1@osiris.wu-wien.ac.at>
           rupert@ifi.uni-klu.ac.at "Rupert THURNER" writes:

> When starting netscape on an osf1 3x alpha we get on certain
> displays the following error:
> 
> Unaligned access pid=8472 <netscape> va=140306c24 pc=120257fec ra=1202571d8
>  type=stq
> 
> continued a minute or so (with different pc) and then it starts up
> normally. if you go to the news-directory, it does the same
> thing again and from this point on you can work normally with it.

This is caused by the program attempting to access a store location
(va=140306c24) which is mis-aligned for its size (therefore size greater
than 4 bytes, which is the alignment). This is normally caused by
accessing a 64 bit pointer which has only been aligned on a 32 bit
boundary (which is all that would be required on a 32 bit machine).

As you say, amazingly programs with this problem still work. I assume
a trap occurs and the mis-aligned access is resolved by software, but
this is just a guess.

> i suppose this is a certain setting of mwm, but i'm not sure, and,
> i have no idea where to search for this error.

No, it's an incorrect/incompleted port of a piece of 32 bit software
to a 64 bit machine (probably). Use dbx and set a breakpoint at the
pc location to find where the problem is in the code. It's probably
something like dynamically allocating space for a structure containing
pointers, but only ensuring 32 bit alignment rather than 64 bit
required on the Alpha.

