Having followed the development of the standard that eventually became Fortran
90, ANSI did not screw up as bad as they could have.  The original concept was
a nice language, but it was not Fortran.  You should have heard the reaction
when a speaker presented the standard at a DECUS symposium.  When the ANSI
group somewhat under-responded to this complaint voting members (including DEC)
brought the earlier standard to a vote; it's defeat woke up the group and
forced them through a two year delay during which the standard got turned back
much more into something resembling historic FORTRAN.

Meanwhile Microsoft, HP, and Sun (others, I'm sure) started advertizing VAX
Fortran compatability.  VAX Fortran has become the defacto industry standard.
I guess its a good thing DEC Fortran is fairly compatable with it.

