In article <14MAR199516211974@mrl.arc.nasa.gov>, ragosta@mrl.arc.nasa.gov 
(ART RAGOSTA) writes:
|>This may be sour grapes, but FORTRAN 90 is a piece of junk.  Before I start
|>a flame war (I was in some of the ANSI meetings on 90 - whew!), let me state
|>that I consider FORTRAN a language for engineers and scientists.  If you
|>want Ada, use Ada!  (Same for C).  I have 200 engineers who barely understand
|>COMMON blocks, let alone the "advanced" features of 90 (and God forbid 95).
|>I can't afford the support fiascos associated with trying to train them.  Also,
|>although 77 is "by definition" 90, the same is NOT true for 95.
|>
|>I think ANSI screwed up.  DEC is not responsible for this (they voted NO on
|>the spec), but please leave 77 alone... and preferably keep upgrading it.

[I've set the followup to be comp.lang.fortran as this is really a
Fortran language issue]

Digital's voting NO on F90 at the ANSI level should not be interpreted as
indicating that Digital does not support the F90 language.  After all,
we also voted NO on F77 and Ada 83 but developed very popular compilers for
those languages.  The NO votes were our way of saying that we thought the
draft standard being voted on were not as good as they could be.  But once
ratified, Digital fully stands behind them.  (We voted YES at the ISO level
because we wanted one standard.)

I don't agree that F90 is "a piece of junk".  Yeah, it's VERY large and has
some language features that were previously unknown in any popular language,
but it also has some nice and elegant features that provide real help for
the kinds of applications Fortran programmers write.  You aren't forced to
use the new features, though you probably already use some that F90
ratifies, such as 31-character identifiers, END DO, etc.

Regardless of what the standards committee decide to throw out for the next
revision, be assured that Digital, like the other major vendors, will continue
support for any "obsolescent" F77 features because we know our customers use
them.
