In article <3k556e$92i@uplherc.upl.com> skj@upl.com "SKJ" writes:

> I looking for information from anyone that has installed an HP 
> JetDirect card in an HP Laserjet and then setup OSF1 to print to it.
> HP has just told me that it is impossible, but I would think that someone
> has probably done it.  Please inform me of anyone that may have created
> a JetDirect driver for OSF1.  Where can I get it? How does it Work?
> 

I have connected an HP4M+ with an HP network card (not sure what sort it
was).
It supported lpd protocol, which simply required adding some entries to
/etc/printcap. You can set up two entries, one with the remote printer
as "text" which dumps out as text, and the other as "raw" which causes
the printer to autosense PostScript or HPLJ.

There are a few snags:
No way to disable the feeble banner page (except with lpr -h each time).
Only a single TCP connection (apparently), so I suggest only a single
host actually transfers to the printer and all other hosts transfer via
the single host. For the same reason I suspect, lpq only works when the
printer is idle - fails to connect if a job is transfering.
"text" mode printing is only 79 characters wide.

If you want to produce a driver to use the HP protocol, I believe all it
does is feed the file in to the printer on TCP port 9100 (from memory).
You will have to add a banner page of your own design if you want them.
I did this on a non-unix box a while back.
