In article <3k72n0$h7v@nz12.rz.uni-karlsruhe.de>,
Thomas Koenig <Thomas.Koenig@ciw.uni-karlsruhe.de> wrote:
>Bob Koehler (koehler@bessta.gsfc.nasa.gov) wrote in comp.lang.fortran,
>>Meanwhile Microsoft, HP, and Sun (others, I'm sure) started advertizing VAX
>>Fortran compatability.  VAX Fortran has become the defacto industry standard.
>
>Does this include things like structures, etc.?  Even in HP-UX 8.0, the
>fort77 - compiler does not support these, nor does IBM's VS compiler, or
>Fujitsu's Fortran compiler vor their VP series. 
"Supports VAX FORTRAN extensions" can mean just about anything.  It's
quite common for the extension to be there and be wrong.  For example,
Sun's structure implementation doesn't always work correctly in some
versions of the Sun FORTRAN compiler, and you won't find out until you run
the program.  We've never seen a FORTRAN that came close to doing it "right"
for all extensions...which is why we had to create a preprocessor which
converts VAX FORTRAN into a target FORTRAN.  Lest one ask, "what about DEC 
FORTRAN for OSF/1"?  It's not perfect either, "same front end" doesn't mean
all the extensions are supported.  It's better in most ways than other Unix
FORTRANs for VMS extensions, though.
--
 Jim Reiss (jim@accelr8.com)    Accelr8 Technology, Denver, Colorado, USA


