In <3k4mop$nvi@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk> jkb@mrc-lmb.cam.ac.uk (James Bonfield) writes:

>I have a C file called (eg) /X/prog.c that includes "inc.h".
>Now there's an "inc.h" in my current directory (which is not /X) and also in
>the /X directory. My current "inc.h" is a different version than the one in
>/X. Now the question is how do I compile prog.c to include my own inc.h? inc.h
>is included using "inc.h" rather than <inc.h>.
>
>The most natural thing is:
>
>cc -I. -c /X/prog.c -o prog.o
>
>However this includes /X/inc.h still. So I then tried disabling the standard
>include path and explicitly setting things:
>
>cc -I -I/usr/include -I`pwd` /X/prog.c -o prog.o
>
>Nope - it still picks up /X/inc.h. I've tried c89 and cc -migrate too. This is
>getting _REALLY_ annoying. I finally figured that 'gcc -I. -I-' works, but I'd
>rather use cc than gcc (as it means recompiling gcc to bring myself vaguely up
>to date).

It's not a bug, it's the documented behaviour.  From the cpp man page:

  -Idir   Change the algorithm for searching for #include files whose names
          do not begin with / to look in dir before looking in the direc-
          tories on the standard list.  Thus, #include files whose names are
          enclosed in "" will be searched for first in the directory of the
          ifile argument, then in directories named in -I options, and last
          in directories on a standard list.  For #include files whose names
          are enclosed in <> , the directory of the ifile argument is not
          searched.

>
>Do I need to use <inc.h> instead of "inc.h"? This will be a real pain if so as
>we're actually talking about in excess of 300 include files that are doubtless
>included many more times.

Apparently, this is the only solution, if you want to avoid gcc.
>
>It seems to be a bug of the cpp to me that it is impossible to override the
>explicit search of the local directory when looking for include files. Is
>there any solution to this other than copying (or symlinking) over all the
>source from /X to .? Seems a bit drastic and annoying.

It's not a bug, it's a feature :-)  You should have thought about this
problem when you developed the code.  The semantics of "" and <> in Unix
are pretty standard, this is not something specific to OSF/1, so you have
no chance to convince DEC to change their cpp.  If you needed the
semantics of <>, you should have used <> in the first place.

Dan
