aksyeo@menudo.uh.edu (Arthur K. S. Yeo) wrote:
>
> Hi folks:
> 
> For those of you who have managed the OSF/1 environment running
> on a generously configured Alpha Server
> (2MB cache, 128 MB RAM, 3GB disk),
> what do think of its stability in terms of the following:
> 
> a) under load (20-30) users running IPC-intensive applications
> b) I/O-bound jobs (like multiple DBMS's running at the same time)
> c) software development
> 
> Somehow, I have been hearing that the mean-time-between-failures
> is quite low compared to VMS, SVR4, Solaris and even Ultrix.
> I have even heard of a case where the log of the 
> Advanced File System was partially destroyed when the system crashed.
> In short, the availability of the system as a whole was not too
> impressive.

Here at SIDOCI, we are running 7 DEC AXP 3000 (600S and 400S)
under DEC OSF/1 v3.0.

One 600S (196 MB RAM, 27 GB on RZ26 and RZ28 drives) is used as
data base and NFS server exclusively.  It runs two INGRES engines
and one STAR server accessing 13 INGRES test DBs totalling 4 GB.
21GB are NFS exported to the other machines.  This system is running
for almost a year and a half now without any major problem.  (Over 
this period is was upgraded from a 400 to a 600, from OSF 1.3, 2.1 to
3.0).

Our development staff is using 3 of the 600S (each with 288 MB RAM
and 1GB swap) with average load of 10-15 users per machine + 3 TUXEDO
testing environments.

All our file systems (expect the root filesystems) are ADVFS.  
We never experienced any loss of data due to crashes.

Stability is very good, especially under 3.0.  We experienced a few
crashes on the NFS client machines when we switched to NFS v3 in early
December.

After applying patches, all systems ran without a glitch
until a power failure in late February.  We had 5 power glitches since
then and we never experienced any loss of data.  Recovery was fast
every time, thanks to ADVFS (image fscking 27GB).

Hope this helps.  


