(Robert Blake) writes:
|>
|>I've been trying to write a shell script that other users can run and appear
|>to be me for the duration of the command. I'm using Alpha OSF/1 v1.3.
|>
|> I've written a very simple shell script called fred:
|>
|>	#!/bin/sh
|>	echo Hi!!
|>	whoami
|>	rm /usr/user1/temp/test~
|>
|>test~ is a file that only user1 has write permission to. Having written this
|>as
|>user1, I then 'chmod 4655 fred' so the permissions look thus:
|>
|>	-rwSr-xr-x   1 user1  physics	117 Mar 07 14:53 fred
|>
|> I then log in as user2 and try to run it. The output is:
|>
|>	Hi!!
|>	user2
|>	rm: override protection 700 for /usr/user1/temp/test~? y
|>	rm: /usr/user1/temp/test~ not removed.  Permission denied
|>
|>Anyone any idea what I'm doing wrong?

Write a C source fred.c as user1:

main()
{
  system("rm /usr/user1/temp/test~");
}

Compile and change mode:

$ cc fred.c -o fred
$ chmod +s fred

Then you can run it as user2.

Walter
