I am wondering if there are ways to fine-tune the parameters OSF/1
uses for TCP/IP traffic in the following situation.

Our campus has two address spaces, 128.8.*.* (with netmask
255.255.255.0) and 129.2.*.* (with netmask 255.255.254.0); I am in the
curious position of being on a subnet in transition between the two,
currently there are hosts with both types of address on the subnet
(e.g. my machine is 128.8.162.74, down the hall there's a machine
129.2.163.16).  I notice that when connecting to other machines across
campus, the 128.8 machines are consistently faster than the 129.2
machines in talking to other 128.8 machines, and the 129.2 machines
are consistently faster than the 128.8 machines in talking to other
129.2 machines.  I'm talking roughly a factor of 2 speed differential
(and let me emphasize, the difference is between connections taking
the exact same path through the campus routers).  All the machines I'm
referring to are DEC Alpha boxes running OSF/1 1.3 or 3.0B (hopefully
all will be running the latter soon).

So I wonder, is it possible that the OS sends packets differently if
the first couple bytes of the addresses of the two hosts match?  If
so, is there a way to tell the OS that 128.8 <-> 129.2 connections are
just as local as 128.8 <-> 128.8 and 129.2 <-> 129.2 connections?  I
am concerned because we are changing over to 129.2 in our building but
the machines I'm interested in a fast connection to are numbered 128.8.

Granted this could be a networking problem and not an OS problem, but
so far the network engineers on campus have been unable to explain
this phenomenon to me.  Many thanks to anyone who can help shed some
light on this effect.

-- 
