Hi folks,
  I had posted a question how to make sendmail on DEC 3000 OSF1 V2.0
240 8-bit-clean, and I was asked to post a summary, because this might
be of interest to many people.

Here it is:

Adding

###################
###   Options   ###
###################

# 8 bit mail
O8

to sendmail.cf, refreezing the configuration file by running
"sendmail -bz" (not implemented on DEC OSF/1), and then restarting
sendmail does the job.

>>> But:

Be aware that the RFC's clearly state that 7-bit data (actually a
*subset* of 7-bit-ascii) is the only thing to be guaranteed to work,
so the next host might (or will) strip your bit #7, thus clobbering
german umlauts.

If transport of 8-bit-data or foreign charsets is what you need you
should move towards MIME. That's another thing: the job is done in the
mail user agent, not the transport agents (sendmail).

So:

I think the best thing is to

> make the own sendmail (and any other you can handle) 8-bit-clean

> not to use umlauts on outgoing mails to the net (as opposed to local
  mails) 

> switch to MIME if binary data transports are needed.

Thanks to all the guys who helped!

Hope this helps,
Winfried
