Andrew Gallatin writes...

>it looks like you need to get a 266 to get the deskside case to get the
>motherboard with the extra PCI slot.

and Andrew apologizes for any confusion.  No need to apologize!  The
names are long and provide ample opportunity for confusion.  But here's
a rough trip guide to how the naming conventions seem to be going, as
I've watched them evolve since their inception 8 months ago:

       AlphaMumble S G/M

Mumble = Station or Server

     S = Rough indicator of size of the box
         Workstations: 200 desktop
                       400 mini-tower
         Servers:  400 mini-tower
                  1000 desk side, about the size of a microwave oven
                  2000 two microwaves 
                  2100 two dishwashers
                  xx00 refrigerator (xx means unannounced, will be > 21)

     G = Chip Generation.  The 21064 at up two 200 Mhz, and the 21064A
         at up to 275 Mhz are both generation 4 (although the "A" indicates 
         a shrink to generation 5 manufacturing capabilities, which is why 
         you may hear us refer to "EV45".)   The 21164, with quad issue,
         much larger on chip-cache, and up to 300 Mhz is generation 5.

     M = Mhz, in all but one case.  A marketing manager who thought that
         customers like round numbers better than exact numbers rounded 
         one of the systems off.  But in all other cases (so far) M is Mhz.

Thus from the above one can derive the likely name of the EV5-based 300
Mhz server previously discussed in this newsgroup and demonstrated at
DECUS last December:

    AlphaServer xx00 5/300

(I'm sorry I can't tell you xx, that's company confidential.)

Meanwhile, back to the original point of this post - I don't think we
have announced any workstations at 266 Mhz.  To get your mini-tower
enclosure with the extra space, you need the 400 series.  Other than the
form factor, there are great similarities between the:

        AlphaStation 200 4/233 - desktop 233 Mhz 
and
        AlphaStation 400 4/233 - mini-tower 233 Mhz

Hope this helps

