Steve Wyeth (stevew@dougal.logica.co.uk) wrote:
: I've inherited a DEC OSF/1 V1.0 machine (a MIPS processor, not an Alpha -
: this confuses DEC every time I ring them about something!).

: I have two installation TK50s, one labeled "Real Time Options" and the other 
: labeled "Volume 2". How many installation tapes should there be? (I would have 
: thought that there is a Volume 1 somewhere!).
: The system is running fine and has done for ages. The other day, though, I
: asked myself the question "How do I restore the root filesystem of this machine
: if the disc crashes?". I have backups of all the filesystems using "dump", so
: restoring data wouldn't be a problem.

: Presumably though, these tapes aren't enough to restore a root filesystem
: (there is likely to be stuff outside the filesystem itself [eg boot code].

: I'd guess that I need to use the installation tapes to recreate
: a root filesystem, then I can restore everything from tape.

: Is this right? Which tapes would I use? Could I boot off the tape device
: (a la VMS)?

: Steve.

Steve, Version 1.0 of OSF/1 was distributed on 3 tapes. They were labeled:

	Installation Software and Mandatory Update PartNo.AQ-PM9RA-BE
	Operating System Volume 2  PartNo.AQ-PM9QA-BE
	Realtime Options  PartNO.AQ-PM9PA-BE 

The first tape is booted to install the whole operating system. In case of a 
crash and you need to rebuild the system disk - YOU NEED THE FIRST TAPE!.

This tape will bring up a memory resident version of OSF and allow you to 
build a root file system so you can restore the rest.

V1.0 was the only offical release of OSF/1 on MIPS platform. It was called a
reference port. The kernel doesn't look anything like what is now running on 
The ALPHA platform. 

Good luck on finding a copy of tape 1.

