As many of you know, we have been working on an OpenVMS AXP version of
our DEC Fortran 90 compiler, which was released on DEC OSF/1 AXP last
summer.  The OpenVMS version is still in beta test, but it's doing so well that
we decided to give folks a chance to try it out for free.  It is installed
on Digital's Internet Alpha demo system, axpvms.pa.dec.com. accessible by
Telnet.  If you already have an account there, the F90 command will get you the 
DEC Fortran 90 compiler. If you don't have an account, log in as axpguest and 
fill out the application you'll be given.

This is the "T2.0-FT2" version of the compiler - not everything works yet, but
the missing features are few and unusual.  The release notes can be found at
SYS$HELP:FORTRAN90.RELEASE_NOTES on the system - please read them before
using the compiler.  HELP F90 is also available.  I'm sorry that we can't
make the manuals available at this time, but the help has language topics
and should suffice.  We expect to begin shipments of V2.0 on OpenVMS AXP in
May; OpenVMS AXP V6.1 will be a required minimum version.

You can't place an order for DEC Fortran 90 for OpenVMS AXP quite yet - but
soon...  It will be sold as a separate product from our Fortran-77 compiler,
DEC Fortran.  It is highly compatible with DEC Fortran, including almost all
of its extensions, but is not a complete superset.  The release notes summarize
some of the differences.  The two may be used side-by-side on a system.

If you have any questions, comments or find bugs not already listed in the 
release notes, please send me the details by mail.

For the UNIX side of the house, axposf.pa.dec.com currently has V1.1 of the
DEC Fortran 90 compiler - we'll have our latest release, V1.3, installed there
within a few days.  Same instructions for account application as above.
