In article <D52oMF.HyH@info.physics.utoronto.ca>, sysmark@helios.physics.utoronto.ca (Mark Bartelt) writes:
|> First of all, I notice that there seems to be some name
|> overloading:  The boot-time message calls the device "fta0"

Since the DEFTA (Turbochannel FDDI) and the DEFEA share everything but the
bus interface, it made sense that they should share as much of the
device driver.  The manual page for "fta" is lagging and should be
updated to reflect this.

|> This being the case, does everything that's
|> documented in that manpage apply equally to a DEFEA?

Yes.

|> The more important question is, how do we enable communication over our
|> FDDI connection?

Did you run "netsetup"?  If you choose item one from its menu (configure
network interfaces) you should notice that it will cycle through all
the available network interfaces in your system.

In the file /etc/rc.config there's a variable called NUM_NETCONFIG.
This should be equal to the number of network interfaces that you
set up via "netsetup."  You can also manually configure the interface
via "ifconfig" (as in "ifconfig fta0 blah, blah") or you can edit
/etc/rc.config by hand and bump NUM_NETCONFIG and fill in NETDEV_X
and IFCONFIG_X appropriately.

fddi_config allows you to tweak certain parameters on the adaptor.  You
probably don't want or need to use this just to get your FDDI running.

If you want the sables to use FDDI exclusively between themselves, then
you may want to create a subnet just for them and connect them all to
a FDDI concentrator.  Then you'd use the names corresponding to the
FDDI's IP addresses as the node names for NFS mounts, etc.  We do this
here in our production environment on our build servers.
