>Alan Hughes <alan.hughes@vegauk.co.uk> wrote:
>
>levy@fndcd.fnal.gov wrote:
>>
>> Convert VMS to OSF?  That's a mistake.  It's like trading your
>> brand-new fully loaded Cadillac for a rusted, broken down, worn
>> out Yugo. 
> 
>
>We did it for one project here, and achieved a performance increase of
>approx *45 times*. What people seem to forget is that VMS is a 32 bit
>OS, OSF is a 32/64 bit OS, and the Alpha processor is 64 bit. Guess
>which OS cannot make best use of the hardware?!
>
>Alan
>

  This is most likely a port from OpenVMS VAX to OSF/1, not a
  comparison of OpenVMS Alpha and OSF/1 on the same hardware.
  Not unless the new code was significantly better, or the old
  code was significantly poorer.

  OSF/1 is a full 64-bit O/S, not a 32/64 O/S.

  OpenVMS Alpha is neither fully 32 or 64 bit.  The hardware itself
  has no "32-bit mode."  It is 64-bit only.  It's math is 64-bit, it's
  virtual addresses are 64-bit.  OpenVMS Alpha has carefully selected
  it's VA mapping, and defaults to allow 32-bit applications to work
  in this environment - without knowing that they are on a 64-bit
  system.  Applications that want 64-bit scalars can get them by
  request.  And OpenVMS is looking to further extend the ability of
  applications to exploit the 64-bit capabilities of the system.

  The performance between OSF/1 and OpenVMS Alpha is within a small
  delta of each other, and they leapfrog each other as compiler changes
  and code optimizations reach each of them.

  Both OSF/1 and OpenVMS Alpha are great O/S's.  Your choice should
  not be driven by some percieved notion that one is faster than the
  other.

