This might very well be true for a special project/application, BUT you 
should ask yorself if the OS is the reason!!


Much SW here is written in PL/I and we found at first the AXPs really slow.
Then we found it's actually the compiler, doing much worse in certain
frequently used functions (esp. ON - units...). So here it was a COMPILER,
not Hardware, and definitely NOT the OS! And if you have a slow response
on the side of the OS, then you should maybe clean up lots of stuff in the 
login-procedures: All the really nice features of chains of logicals etc.
available in VMS sometimes tend to blow up the Login-procedures very much, 
especcially if VMS has been used for a looong time...
No such 'dust' piled up over more than 10 years will be found on a newly 
installed OSF, of course!


Others might be fitter than me to comment on 32, 32/64 or 64 bit OS in 
both cases. Id really be interested in the answers on this....

