In article <3jfr1k$nj7$1@mhadg.production.compuserve.com>, Ewald Muessig <100520.237@CompuServe.COM> writes:
> Hi, 
> 
> I am using a DEC AXP running OSF/1 V2.0
> 
> I try to connect PC's running FTPs PCTCP V2.2 and SunSoft
> PC-NFS V5.0. Using both NFS products I cannot mount a 
> OSF/1 disk from the pc. I always get an access denied error 
> message. I can mount this disk from any other Unix System like
> Sun4, Solaris, HP-UX, AIX.
> 
> The filesystem is exported for mount from anybody on OSF/1
> 

Check the file /etc/rc.config, and make sure that the variable NONROOTMOUNTS
is set to 1.  The default is 0, which will deny PC's access to the NFS
mounts.  This drove me crazy for quite some time befor I figured it out.
(I know, RTFM. :(

I KNOW this to be true under OSF/1 V1.3.  I'm fairly certain it's true under
OSF/1 V3.0.  I don't have root access to a machine running OSF/1 V2.0, or a
DOC set for OSF/1 Vany handy, so I can't go check it out.  But try setting
that, either via nfssetup, or by editing /etc/rc.config and rebooting.

Good luck
